<%#
  THE YAML DRAWER (2026-09-09; spec §2, §4). Hidden until "Edit as text"; beside the page, never
  over it, so a drag on the left is watched on the right. The text is whatever the server rendered
  for the current draft (Projects::LayoutsController#yaml); Apply posts it back (#parse) and the
  controller adopts the draft that returns. Copy is the shared copy controller with its text
  value kept current by the editor.

  THE MAKER READS "TEXT", NEVER "YAML" (2026-11-02, the UX audit's §H). The format is still YAML and
  the code still says so; what the drawer tells a maker is what it is for — copy a layout out, paste
  one in.
%>
<aside class="layout-drawer" hidden data-layout-editor-target="drawer" aria-label="Layout as text">
  <div class="layout-drawer-head">
    <b>Layout as text</b>
    <span>
      <button type="button" class="btn small" data-controller="copy" data-copy-text-value=""
              data-action="copy#copy" data-layout-editor-target="copy">Copy</button>
      <%# QUIET, NOT PRIMARY (2026-10-26): Apply never writes — Save in the bar does — and the edit
          area spends its accent once per tab, on that Save (design-system.md rule 13; pinned for this
          page in accent_spend_test.rb, which counts the drawer while it is closed too). %>
      <button type="button" class="btn small" data-action="layout-editor#apply">Apply</button>
    </span>
  </div>
  <%# `input->yamlTyped` keeps Copy honest against hand-typed text: the copy value would otherwise be
      the server's last render, not what is on screen (found in review). %>
  <textarea class="layout-yaml" rows="24" spellcheck="false" aria-label="Layout as text"
            data-layout-editor-target="yaml" data-action="input->layout-editor#yamlTyped"></textarea>
  <div class="layout-drawer-notes" data-layout-editor-target="notes" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="muted">Copy this text to reuse the layout in another project, or paste one here. Apply updates the page behind this drawer; nothing is saved until Save layout. A layout naming a folder this project lacks is applied without that entry, and says so here.</p>
</aside>
